From: Andrew S Hall <ashall@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: Re: PHIL: 'Western' Rationalism

The numerous influences of Semetic, African, and other culture on the
Ancient Greeks remains hotly (and often, stupidly) contested. There
is one thing that really can't be disputed. Rationalism as an approach
to knowledge was indigenously Greek. They may have taken aspects of
astronomy/astology from the Babylonians or mathematics from the Egyptians,
but they removed the religous and mystical framework and made the study
of these subjects an end in itself.

(I am not claiming no other cultures have achieved this step, but
that the Greeks did it on their own and are hence the birth of the
tradition of Western rationalism)

Andrew

