From: pcm@cs.brown.edu (Peter C. McCluskey)
Subject: Re: PHIL: Electrons and Consciousness
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute freely

 davidc@cs.uwa.edu.au  (David Cake) writes:
>	For another possibly good reference, anybody read the new Daniel
>Dennett book, modestly titled 'Consciouness Explained'. If anybody has, I 
>wouldn't mind a review, especially from someone with a philosophy background.
>Max More maybe?

 It's a fairly good book. He isn't serious about the title; he knows
that he is just refuting some of the mistaken theories and making a
few steps in the right direction. He shows fairly conclusively that
there is no centralized "Cartesian Theater" that controls thoughts
or which determines whether a perception becomes part of consciousness.
He describes his alternative as a pandemonium model, where numerous
agents shout out, and one gets heard at any given time, creating a
"virtual uniprocessor" simulated on very parallel hardware. Almost
a free market theory of the mind, trying to replace socialist theories,
except that there is no pricing mechanism proposed.
 I don't have much of a philosophical background. All that I remember
of that part of the book was a fairly convincing argument against
the idea of qualia.

