Date: Thu, 21 Jan 93 10:49:50 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Re: I&A: Hip PC/Call for calls

Doug Platt writes a long article about the Hip-PC, about developing
businesses, and about the comments of several Extropians.

For brevity, text elided.

> One area of discussion was the social acceptability of wearing your computer. 
> This is a serious concern, but there are so many markets in which it doesn't 
> matter, that I don't think it is a showstopper. This was basically Nick's 
> conclusion. He listed military, medical, industrial and other applications as 
> promising target markets - and these are indeed some of the markets I expect 

As others have noted, wearing lots of high-tech gear has severe
negative connotations in most parts of our society--unless you're a
half-naked Madonna with a headset microphone or a camcorder-toting
t.v. person. Sliderules worn on the belt used to mark the nerds (I
wore one very briefly in 1969), then calculators did the same thing.

This _may_ change. And of course some folks just won't care.

> Another way of dealing with the social acceptability issue is to use text to 
> speech with a tiny earphone as the output device rather than the Private Eye. 
> This would work fine for a business man in a meeting who wants to take notes, 
> and reference spreadsheet data. 

The businessman who wants to take notes and reference spreadsheet data
in a meeting simply hauls his laptop out of his briefcase and
proceeds. It is doubtful he needs a Hip-PC or Private Eye kind of
setup. In fact, laptops in meetings are becoming the norm, while
putting on a "gargoyle suit" (Stephenson's term in "Snow Crash") may
make the budding executive look absurd. (I'm not saying he _is_
absurd-looking, just noting the downside risks.)

> Somehow or another we're going to have to deal with the social responses to 
> wearing gear if we expect to start taking STAYING ALIVE more seriously. I 
> just bought a bicycle helmet to wear while I drive for additional head 
> protection in a crash, and to experiment with mounting the Private Eye. 

Here I _really_ differ with Doug! The best way to _stay alive_ while
driving a car or riding a bicycle is to be alert and to eschew
cellular phones, gargoyle suits, and other distractions while driving
or riding. This is the same response several of us gave to Tom Morrow
when he was talking about the need for such computing devices for his
commute driving once he arrives in California.

It is hard to imagine any data accessible on a Hip-PC that would help
a driver to "stay alive." The relatively small number of bits that may
help, such as traffic reports, weather conditions, etc., are trivially
broadcast in radio reports, the original "heads-up communication system."

> As to chording being something that will take some effort to become 
> accepted....that's why I've had TryChorder written. Soon, you'll all be able 
> to make your own conclusions about this form of input.

I'm in the market for a new kind of keyboard or entry device (for my
Mac IIci and PowerBook 100), so I'll be following Doug's work with
interest. But mainly I want it for speed. 

> Garret wrote something about my company, Select Tech, Inc. becoming the hub 
> of a wheel of interconnected extropian companies, while Tim (citing Perry) 
> mentioned something to the effect that serious business is not going to be 
> discussed in such a public forum. Well, just for the record, let me state 

Yes, I cited Perry's comments, though I don't fully agree with them. I
suspect most businesses fail for reasons other than that the ideas
leaked out in a public forum and somebody else beat them to the
market. (Perry will perhaps agree with this.)

The problems with discussing new ideas for businesses over this list
are manifold:

1. Low bandwidth. We just can't brainstorm, educate each other, draw
pictures, crunch numbers in front of a computer screen, have wild
arguments, go out for pizza and beer, and all the othe things people
starting companies do.

2. Widely differing viewpoints and objectives. We come together on
this list for a variety of reasons, some ideological, some due to
interests in technology or politics, and so on. We are not, however,
as similar as folks in some company might be who see a chance to form
a company.

3. Physical separation. Related to #1 above, but worth mentioning
separately. That we are spread across the U.S. (mostly), and even
across the world, makes for difficult face-to-face meetings.

> did I dream that? All seriousness aside, I would like to work with others on 
> this list, but it is a tricky thing. Being a fan of Harry Browne's HOW I 
> FOUND FREEDOM IN AN UNFREE WORLD, I am always on my guard for what he calls 

Yes! This book is one of my all-time favorites.

> "group traps". I prefer to work with people as individual subcontractors and 
> would like to avoid ever having to have employees. Yet most potential 
> investors want to see a business plan that assumes a traditional 
> organizational structure. And the problem with attempting to only work with 
> independent contracters is that negotiations become ongoing and complex. Any 
> ideas on this? Similarly, the kinds of deals I favor with potential 
> money-sellers (investors) are non-traditional and creative and possibly 
> overly complex. I would like to keep the whole thing self-financing. We'll 
> see.

I know of half a dozen groups planning startups. So many folks are
talking startups that I've taken to reading Gordon Bell's book on
high-tech startups and crunching some numbers in my own spreadsheet
models (if it's of interest, in "SpreadBase," a new object-oriented
spreadsheet-database from Objective Software, a company started by a
friend of mine...which has raised more than $10 M).

> The year is 1995 and wearable computers with hmd and chordic input have taken> off. 

Doubtful, for the various reasons cited by me and others. 1994 in only
two years away, barely time for a new product category to get out on
store shelves (unless it "explodes into a niche" like the Sony Walkman
did in 1979-1981), much less to have "taken off." The Apple Newton
PDA, Eo unit, and General Magic unit have all had gestation periods of
several years, for example.

I don't want to sound "dynamically unoptimistic," but competing with
these folks, with the several dozen laptop makers (and remember that
laptops are as effective as Hip-PC-type gadgets for most business
applications, perhaps more so, and have fewer of the stigmas and
obstacles to overcome), and with the well-funded "VideoVision" startup
in Washington state (big ads in the "San Jose Mercury News" seeking
engineers) will be tough.

The products which _may_ take off by 1994 are likely to come from
projects that are already very far along, that have large enough
staffs to do the various things that need to be done (technological,
marketing, legal, factory, etc.). 

At this point, it might make sense to start targeting the products for
the 1996-8 period, as these are _already_ being brainstormed by many
companies. (This doesn't mean there won't be come-from-nowhere
companies with good ideas that get started late and nevertheless
succeed brilliantly. Perhaps Doug's company will be one of them.)

However, I wish Doug all the best.

-Tim

