It's a very wide question, and would require a long time for a full answer,
even if I knew about any of these things in detail.

A few brief pointers:

You already mentioned one technique: smart drugs.  Books, sources, and
personal experiences with them have appeared here.  Similarly for
anti-aging techniques.  Most of what I've seen on anti-aging is just ways
of taking better care of your body, rather than going for large increases
in life-span.  This is probably because no-one knows how to do the latter
yet.

Cryonics: I take it everyone here knows of Alcor?  Several list members are
signed up with them.  See also the cryonics mailing list and sci.cryonics.

Intelligence enhancement and such: are you reading the current debate about
EST and Forum?

A while back Richard Johnson posted a long list of book references covering
a number of the topics you list, and others.  The great majority dealt with
exactly the sort of self-improvement methods that you are particularly
interested in.  It doesn't include much evaluation of their merits, but he
said that he would welcome informed comments about them, and would update
the list to reflect the emerging consensus.  I don't know if he has
actually received many comments.

Synchronicity strikes: a message from Nick Szabo just arrived, describing
his use of dietary supplements.

So you see, there's no shortage of material here already on here-and-now
techniques.  All you have to do is read it.  And as with any mailing list
or newsgroup, if there's anything else you want to see discussed, all you
have to do is discuss it.

- - - - - - - -

My own somewhat sceptical view of diets, supplements, and medical advice
about nutrition, is that I eat and drink whatever I want.  When I see some
new dietary scare I just think "Look what they're telling us now" and
dismiss it.  The only major illness I've ever had resulted, I believe, from
not following that principle.

I have mixed feelings about a lot of the self-improvement stuff.  Book
titles like "How to be Twice as Smart" set my bogosity detector pinging. 
On the other hand, some of the dismissive comments I've recently seen here
about Forum seem to me like saying that doctors are worthless because
healthy people who know how to take care of themselves don't need them.

