Date: Thu, 21 Jan 93 15:19:36 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: CORP: shareholder interests

   Perry Metzger:
   > In any case, it appears that you have a very weak notion of what the
   > purpose of a company is. Its to maximize profit for investors, period.
   > It has no duty to employees or "the public" or anyone else -- its goal
   > is properly maximization of total profit on the capital invested in the
   > venture.

The subtlty of the idea is that single-minded pursuit of profit is
likely to be sub-optimal in the amount of profit produced.  This is
because profit is easily measured, while the value of goodwill
destroyed in the pursuit of profit is hard to measure.  Thus,
companies that are too obsessed with profit are likely to destroy
their long-run profitability by destroying the essential asset of
goodwill in those they deal with.  That's why there is the sudden
proliferation of blather about "taking care of all the stakeholders"
of a business -- it prevents the expenditure of necessary karma points
in the production of (short-term) profit.

Consider the case of Eastern Airlines, where the company managed to
lose so much goodwill with its employees that the employees destroyed
their own jobs in vengance.  Lorenzo *thought* he was maximizing
profit, but he surely wasn't.

Over the long-term, most "morality" is identical to "self-interest".

Dale

